# The woven corpus

Weave produces one value for one corpus: a `WovenCorpus`. It holds a whole corpus as data — a navigation tree of the corpus's parts and chapters, and a page for each chapter that carries its prose and its code in reading order. `WeaveBuilder` fills it from the parsed corpus, and whoever renders it reads it from there. Nothing else crosses between the two.

The model says what a corpus contains, never how a site around it looks. Prose stays Markdown source, code stays its own text, and an anchor stays a link waiting to be drawn. The model carries no chrome — no top menu, no theme, no chosen sidebar — because those belong to whoever renders it. A top menu, in particular, spans several corpora that no single corpus knows about, so it can never come from this data. A consumer reads one `WovenCorpus`, or several side by side, and builds its own navigation over them.

So the model is framework-agnostic on purpose. `loom weave` writes the data; the presentation is free. Swap the presentation — a different framework, a different theme, a different menu — and the projection does not move.

## Built on Effect Schema

Like every Loom model, a woven corpus is an Effect `Schema.Struct`, so one definition both validates the data and gives it a type.

=>

import { Schema } from 'effect'

const intAtLeast = (min: number) =>
  Schema.makeFilter<number>((n) =>
    Number.isInteger(n) && n >= min ? undefined : `must be an integer >= ${min}`,
  )

## The source of a block

Weave keeps every block tied to the loom it came from. A `SourceAnchor` is that tie: the `chapter` slug that names the block's page, and the `section` heading it was written under. Both are the same slug and heading id the page and its links already use, so an anchor names a place a reader can already reach.

The anchor turns the rendered page back into the source. A consumer carries it onto the element it draws, and a reader who points at that element points at the loom section behind it — the spec — not merely a line of rendered HTML. The annotation utility reads the anchor to pin a remark to the source, where a re-weave and edits around it cannot shift it. The whole section is the unit, so the pin holds as long as the heading does.

=>

export const SourceAnchorSchema = Schema.Struct({
  chapter: Schema.String,
  section: Schema.String,
})
export type SourceAnchor = typeof SourceAnchorSchema.Type

## Links between sections

Within a code section an author writes `::[A greeting]` to draw in another section. Tangle inlines the named section; weave keeps the reference and turns it into a link. An `AnchorLink` is that resolved reference. It carries the `name` the author wrote, the page and heading it points to, and the span it occupies in the source — so the presentation can make just those characters a link.

An anchor may also reach a section that holds no code yet, or name one nobody has declared under the `{To be settled}` label, as the chapter "Names through the book" describes. An `UnsettledSpan` marks such an anchor's span within its code, so the page can mark the reference as to be settled. An anchor that names nothing yet has no link at all, so the mark stands on its own.

=>

export const AnchorLinkSchema = Schema.Struct({
  name: Schema.String,
  targetSlug: Schema.String,
  targetId: Schema.String,
  offset: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(0)),
  length: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(0)),
})
export type AnchorLink = typeof AnchorLinkSchema.Type

export const UnsettledSpanSchema = Schema.Struct({
  offset: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(0)),
  length: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(0)),
})
export type UnsettledSpan = typeof UnsettledSpanSchema.Type

## The blocks of a page

A page is a sequence of blocks, in the order the author wrote them. A block is one of five, and only a discussion holds the fifth. Prose is a run of Markdown, held as its Markdown source. A heading carries its level, its title, the `id` a link targets, and whether its section is `unsettled`: declared and named by a composing anchor, but holding no code yet. A heading that a divider wrote, rather than a run of `#` marks, is a `divider`, which the presentation draws as a rule with the title at its centre. Code carries its language, its verbatim `code`, the anchor links found within it, and the `unsettled` spans of the anchors in it that reach no code. It also keeps the syntax that framed it: the `arrow` that opened it, as written, and whether a tilde `closed` it or it ran on to the next heading. Neither belongs to the code, but a reader learning Loom needs to see where a block began and ended, so the presentation can draw them around it. A note is a reader's annotation — its Markdown body holds the quoted fragment and the comment — kept apart from the prose so the editor can list it and, once addressed, remove it. A note the author named carries that name as its `caption`, which the page shows above the note.

The fifth block marks where a message of a discussion begins. In a discussion a tilde opens each message, and the name written after the tilde signs it, so a `message` block carries that name as its `author`. The message's words follow as blocks of their own. A message nobody signed carries no author.

Every block also carries the `source` anchor of the section it came from, so the reference back to the loom rides with the block wherever the presentation puts it.

=>

export const ProseBlockSchema = Schema.Struct({
  type: Schema.tag('prose'),
  source: SourceAnchorSchema,
  markdown: Schema.String,
})
export type ProseBlock = typeof ProseBlockSchema.Type

export const HeadingBlockSchema = Schema.Struct({
  type: Schema.tag('heading'),
  source: SourceAnchorSchema,
  level: Schema.Number.check(intAtLeast(1)),
  title: Schema.String,
  id: Schema.String,
  unsettled: Schema.Boolean,
  divider: Schema.Boolean,
})
export type HeadingBlock = typeof HeadingBlockSchema.Type

export const CodeBlockSchema = Schema.Struct({
  type: Schema.tag('code'),
  source: SourceAnchorSchema,
  language: Schema.String,
  code: Schema.String,
  links: Schema.Array(AnchorLinkSchema),
  unsettled: Schema.Array(UnsettledSpanSchema),
  arrow: Schema.String,
  closed: Schema.Boolean,
})
export type CodeBlock = typeof CodeBlockSchema.Type

export const NoteBlockSchema = Schema.Struct({
  type: Schema.tag('note'),
  source: SourceAnchorSchema,
  caption: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  markdown: Schema.String,
})
export type NoteBlock = typeof NoteBlockSchema.Type

export const MessageBlockSchema = Schema.Struct({
  type: Schema.tag('message'),
  source: SourceAnchorSchema,
  author: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
})
export type MessageBlock = typeof MessageBlockSchema.Type

export const WovenBlockSchema = Schema.Union([
  ProseBlockSchema,
  HeadingBlockSchema,
  CodeBlockSchema,
  NoteBlockSchema,
  MessageBlockSchema,
])
export type WovenBlock = typeof WovenBlockSchema.Type

## A page for each chapter

Each chapter becomes one `WovenPage`: a `slug` for its URL, the chapter `title`, and its `blocks`. When the chapter belongs to a part, the page names that part, so the reader sees it above the title, and when it stands in a named book, the page names that book too. When the chapter opens on frontmatter, the page also carries that block verbatim, its `---` fences included, so a reader can be shown the chapter's place in the book and the package and language it writes, the way its loom writes them.

=>

export const WovenPageSchema = Schema.Struct({
  slug: Schema.String,
  title: Schema.String,
  part: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  book: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  frontmatter: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  blocks: Schema.Array(WovenBlockSchema),
})
export type WovenPage = typeof WovenPageSchema.Type

## The navigation tree

The navigation tree is the corpus's own structure — its parts, and the chapters grouped under them, in the order the book's contents set. A consumer renders it into whatever navigation it wants, a sidebar or a drop-down or nothing at all; the model only carries the shape. A `WovenNavEntry` is one chapter in that tree: its `number` — empty when the chapter is numberless — its `title`, the `slug` that opens it, the optional `status` and `priority` a roadmap reads, and the optional `language` its frontmatter names, which a menu can mark the chapter by. A `WovenPart` groups the entries that share a part, under the part's `number` — empty for a numberless part — and its `name`. A chapter that belongs to no part is an orphan, and the orphans form one more numberless part after the others, named `Orphan chapters`, so the body ends on them.

The book's discussions come last, as its back matter. They form one more `WovenPart`, numberless and named `Back matter`, and each of its entries opens a discussion, in the order the book's contents set.

A corpus of several books gives each named book a tree of its own. A `WovenBook` carries the book's `name`, the `slug` of the page its book loom opens, empty when no loom opens it, and the book's own `nav`, built the same way as the default book's.

=>

export const WovenNavEntrySchema = Schema.Struct({
  number: Schema.String,
  title: Schema.String,
  slug: Schema.String,
  status: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  priority: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  language: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
})
export type WovenNavEntry = typeof WovenNavEntrySchema.Type

export const WovenPartSchema = Schema.Struct({
  number: Schema.String,
  name: Schema.String,
  chapters: Schema.Array(WovenNavEntrySchema),
})
export type WovenPart = typeof WovenPartSchema.Type

export const WovenBookSchema = Schema.Struct({
  name: Schema.String,
  slug: Schema.String,
  nav: Schema.Array(WovenPartSchema),
})
export type WovenBook = typeof WovenBookSchema.Type

## The corpus

A `WovenCorpus` is the corpus as data: the `nav` of its default book, the named `books` with their own trees, and the `pages` a reader reads. A corpus of one book names no book, so its `books` list is empty and its menu reads as it always has.

=>

export const WovenCorpusSchema = Schema.Struct({
  nav: Schema.Array(WovenPartSchema),
  books: Schema.Array(WovenBookSchema),
  pages: Schema.Array(WovenPageSchema),
})
export type WovenCorpus = typeof WovenCorpusSchema.Type

